C = [
("h1", "6. The Image and the Firstborn"),
("p", "Claim: “firstborn of every creature” is a title of rank, and the verses that follow it show that it is not a statement that Christ was the first thing created. The volume argues this from the passage itself, and keeps the use of the word for Israel and the use of it for Christ apart."),
("sub", "6.1 “The image of the invisible God”"),
("p", "“Who is the image of the invisible God” (Colossians 1:15). The sentence says that God is invisible, and that Christ is his image. The two statements need no more help to be read. An image is something in which what cannot be seen is seen. John Piper reads the verse in that sense: “He is the visible God.” The volume reports the sentence and the reading, and notes that Paul does not say here how the image is related to the Father in a way that settles every later question. He says that Christ is the image of the invisible God, and that is what the sentence gives."),
("sub", "6.2 “The firstborn of every creature”"),
("p", "The next phrase has been used to say that Christ is the first of the creatures. The sentence does not say so, and the verse that follows shows why: “For by him were all things created, that are in heaven, and that are in earth” (Colossians 1:16). The reason given for the title is that he created all things. If he is a creature, he is one of the things created by him, and the reason does not fit the title. The sentence is therefore read as a title of rank, and the explanation in the next verse is that he stands before and above all created things."),
("p", "Thomas Constable states the point: “What firstborn does not mean is that Christ was the first created being.” He adds, “Christ was before all creation in time, and He is over all creation in authority.” John Piper gives the same point: the word “does not mean he is part of creation.” Both writers read the title as rank. The volume agrees, and the reason is the verse that follows the title."),
("sub", "6.3 What the word “firstborn” can mean"),
("p", "Constable notes that the word “may denote either priority in time (the first child born) or supremacy in rank.” The volume holds that the passage settles the sense by its own verses, and does not need to choose between the two by the dictionary. Two passages from the Scriptures that Paul’s readers knew show the second sense. In Psalm 89:27 the LORD says of the king he will raise up, “Also I will make him my firstborn, higher than the kings of the earth.” The firstborn is made so, and the sense is higher than the kings. In Exodus 4:22 the LORD says of a nation, “Israel is my son, even my firstborn.” The nation is not the first of the nations to be born. The word names a standing."),
("sub", "6.4 Israel’s firstborn and Christ’s"),
("p", "The two uses should be kept apart. Israel is the LORD’s firstborn in Exodus 4:22, and the title belongs to the nation and its king in the Scriptures of Israel. That use is read in the study on sonship, which treats it as Israel’s and does not give it to the Body. Christ is the firstborn in Colossians 1:15 and 1:18, and in Romans 8:29, where God predestinated those he foreknew “to be conformed to the image of his Son, that he might be the firstborn among many brethren.” That is a second use, and it is Christ’s."),
("p", "The volume does not say that the two are the same title in the same sense, and it does not say that the Body receives Israel’s sonship by reason of Christ’s firstborn standing. Colossians 1 says the title is his, and the standing is for the whole of creation. Romans 8:29 says the brethren are conformed to the image of the Son. It does not say they receive the title Israel has. The line holds in both directions: the Scriptures of Israel use the word for Israel, and Paul uses it for Christ."),
("ctbl", "The word “firstborn” in the passages the volume uses.", [
    ("The passage", "Of whom", "What the sentence leaves unsaid"),
    ("Exodus 4:22 “Israel is my son, even my firstborn”", "The nation Israel", "Whether the title implies an order of birth; it names a standing before Pharaoh"),
    ("Psalm 89:27 “my firstborn, higher than the kings of the earth”", "The king, David’s line", "How the king is “made” firstborn; the verse says higher than the kings"),
    ("Colossians 1:15 “the firstborn of every creature”", "Christ", "Whether it denotes time or rank; the next verse bears on it"),
    ("Colossians 1:18 “the firstborn from the dead”", "Christ", "The relation to the other resurrections"),
    ("Romans 8:29 “the firstborn among many brethren”", "Christ", "What sonship the brethren have; the verse says conformed to the image"),
  ], [0.40, 0.20, 0.40]),
("sub", "6.5 “The firstborn from the dead”"),
("p", "Four verses later the title returns with a qualifier: “And he is the head of the body, the church: who is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead; that in all things he might have the preeminence” (Colossians 1:18). The second use is a standing among those raised, and the sentence gives its purpose: “that in all things he might have the preeminence.” The title in verse 15 is read as rank from the verse that follows it, and the title in verse 18 is followed by a purpose clause about preeminence. In both places the title sits in a sentence about his place over others. The order of the resurrections, and where the Body’s rising falls in it, is the subject of the volume on the resurrection order, and this volume takes only the point that the title is used of his standing."),
("sub", "6.6 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "“The image of the invisible God” says that in Christ the invisible God is seen, and the sentence goes no further.",
    "“Firstborn of every creature” is a title of rank, and the reason is the verse that follows: by him all things were created.",
    "Constable and Piper both read the title as rank and reject the reading that makes him the first created being.",
    "Israel’s firstborn standing is Israel’s, and Christ’s is his own; no sonship passes to the Body by this title.",
  ]),
("p", "The title of rank leaves open how the Creator and the image are one. The volume leaves it open, as Paul does. The next chapter takes the verses on creation."),
]
